  "image_name": "b4ede1cce779445a7be41f0a9db4376d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a tectonic evolution from about 50 to 10 million years ago during closure of the Tethyan ocean between Africa/Arabia and Eurasia. Across all panels, a blue oceanic slab subducts northward beneath Eurasia, while orange-red material rises from below and interacts with mantle circulation.\n\nEarly on, subduction produces a narrow zone of surface subsidence above the trench and broad mantle flow above the 660 km discontinuity. In the next stage, the slab penetrates deeper and becomes anchored, while upwelling beneath Africa strengthens and seems to feed volcanism and regional uplift on the African side, contrasted with continued subsidence near Eurasia.\n\nAs Arabia approaches collision, rifting begins and a horizontal “conveyor belt” of entrained mantle flow develops beneath the lithosphere, moving from the upwelling region toward the trench. Dynamic topography shifts to broader uplift over Arabia/Africa, while the trench advances and the slab remains attached at depth.\n\nIn the final stage, Arabia breaks up and collides, and the slab appears to break off. The figure emphasizes how changing slab geometry, deep anchoring, mantle upwelling, and lateral mantle transport together control uplift, subsidence, volcanism, and the transition from oceanic subduction to continental collision.",
